comments.comment:
'You anointed' then 'my head with oil' (Psalm 23:5; LXX 22:5).

In many places the head signifies the mind;

as when it is said:

'The wise man's eyes are in his head' (Eccl 2:14).

Indeed, common usage also says that the more foolish have no brain, have no head.

The ruler of a system is called the head of the system.

Since then the mind also rules and leads our whole composition, it is called head.

… He did not say simply 'was anointed,' but 'with oil';

for there is also worthless oil, concerning which the holy one prays, saying:

'let not the oil of the sinner anoint my head' (Psalm 141:5; LXX 140:5).

… Not less than the other scriptures, the verses set before us also posit the Lord as provider of all, with human pursuits becoming vain if they do not obtain the fitting end from God;

hence, doing anything, let us not give the whole to ourselves, but to the perfecting God, nor, having left all to him, let us end up inactive.

For the verses set before us declare both—then from us and from God—saying vain is the one who builds without God, not the one who does not work at all.
